[{:db/id 17592186354979 :question/problem "Let $v_1=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $v_2=\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$. Form $P=(v_1\\ v_2)$ using these columns in this order, with their displayed scales unchanged. For the differential equation $x'(t)=Ax(t)$, define the new coordinates by $u(t)=Dx(t)$ where $D=P^{-1}$. Find the exact matrix $D$." :question/worked-solution "Use the given eigenvectors as the columns of $P$ in the stated order and with their shown scales:\n\n$$P=(v_1\\ v_2)=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-3\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\det P=1\\cdot2-(-3)(-1)=-1.$$\n\nThe requested coordinate map is $u=Dx$ with $D=P^{-1}$. The inverse formula gives\n\n$$D=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}2&3\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&-3\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nBoth $DP$ and $PD$ are the identity matrix, so this is the exact inverse. If $Av_i=\\lambda_i v_i$, then $AP=P\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ and\n\n$$u'=P^{-1}Ax=P^{-1}APu=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)u.$$\n\nThe opposite coordinate direction is $x=Pu$, whose matrix is $P$, not $D$. Fixing column order and eigenvector scales is necessary to specify this exact inverse. For example, $-D$ also yields a decoupled system if one instead uses the rescaled coordinates $\\widetilde u=-u$, but it is not $P^{-1}$ for the specified $P$. The original generic phrase “the decoupling matrix” did not state these conventions. The clarified prompt makes the exact inverse unambiguous; the source accepted this inverse as option c." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
